{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## The finding\n\nEpic 6202's story **S1 (`aa40-cbda`, \"Isolate review-gate scratch on a dedicated EBS volume\") is\nCLOSED, but its deliverable is not in effect.** The volume exists, is attached, and is raw and\nunmounted; gate scratch is running on the ROOT filesystem, which is precisely the silent fallback\nS1 exists to prevent.\n\n## Measured on the live host, 2026-09-05 (SSM, read-only)\n\n```\nlsblk\n  nvme1n1   50G  xfs   /var/gerrit   vol06fa2e77a9dd97527\n  nvme0n1   60G        (root)        vol0270fcf13709cf472\n  nvme2n1   50G  ----   ----         vol06780b8557d1416b7   <- NO FSTYPE, NO MOUNTPOINT\n\nblkid /dev/nvme2n1            -> none            (no filesystem at all)\n/etc/fstab                    -> entries for /, /boot/efi, /var/gerrit ONLY; no gate-scratch line\ndu -sh /var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch -> 9.3G       ON ROOT, actively written (mtime within 2 min)\nls -A  /var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch -> rebar-gate-snapshots, reviewbot-nopn30zv,\n                                      reviewbot-t8kxn0i8, semgrep-mcp\ndf -h /                       -> 34G used of 60G (57%)\n.gate-scratch-required        -> ABSENT\n.gate-scratch-mounted         -> ABSENT\n```\n\nAWS confirms the volume is `in-use`, attached to `i-00880b2c7f13527c5` at `/dev/sdg`, and the NVMe\nserial on `nvme2n1` is exactly `vol06780b8557d1416b7`. So there is no ambiguity about which device\nit is — it simply was never formatted or mounted.\n\n## Why it happened\n\nThe volume was created by a `terraform apply` **after** the instance's first boot. `user_data.sh`\nruns under cloud-init on FIRST BOOT ONLY, so its `mount_ebs_volume \"${gate_scratch_volume_id}\"`\nstep has never executed for this volume. Nothing else on the host mounts it. This is a real gap in\nthe delivery path, not a bug in `user_data.sh`'s logic: the script is correct and simply never ran.\n\n## The monitoring worked — and is worth crediting\n\nBoth alarms are correctly in ALARM right now:\n\n- `rebar-gate-scratch-unmounted` — `gate_scratch_mounted` reads 0.\n- `rebar-gate-scratch-disk-high` — its metric `disk_used_percent` publishes NOTHING (there is no\n  mount to `df`), and because `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"` it alarms rather than reading as\n  healthy. This is exactly the dead-man-correct behaviour `bff5-9163` was closed for.\n\nSo this was detectable and detected. What was missing is that S1 closed on code correctness without\na check that the volume actually entered service.\n\n## Why the marker design means this is DEGRADED, not BROKEN\n\n`user_data.sh:123-130` places two markers on deliberately different filesystems:\n`.gate-scratch-required` beside the mount point on ROOT (the declaration), `.gate-scratch-mounted`\ninside the mount point on the VOLUME (the proof). Declaration present + proof absent makes gate\nadmission REFUSE rather than quietly repopulate on root.\n\nHere **both** are absent, so admission does not refuse — gates run on root. That is the degraded\npath, and it has evidently been running for days. It is stable, which is why this is not a p1: root\nis at 57% with 26G free. But it means the isolation S1 bought is not being had, and a gate run that\nfills root now takes Gerrit down with it — the exact coupling S1 removed on paper.\n\n## Remediation is NOT a simple mount — read this before acting\n\n`/var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch` currently holds **9.3G of live, in-use working directories**\n(`reviewbot-*` sandboxes, gate snapshots, semgrep state). Mounting the volume over that path HIDES\nthose contents from any process that opens them afterwards, while leaving the 9.3G stranded on root.\nDoing it while a gate or review is mid-run would break that run.\n\nThe contents are genuinely scratch, so no data migration is needed — but a QUIET WINDOW is, and the\nstranded copy must be removed afterwards or the space is lost twice. Sequence:\n\n1. Confirm no gate/review is in flight (the review-bot is the main consumer).\n2. `mkfs.xfs /dev/nvme2n1` — safe, `blkid` confirms no filesystem, serial confirms the device.\n3. `mkdir -p /var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch` (exists), append\n   `UUID=<uuid> /var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch xfs defaults,nofail 0 2` to `/etc/fstab`, `mount -a`.\n4. Assert `mountpoint -q /var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch` BEFORE creating any marker.\n5. `touch /var/lib/rebar/.gate-scratch-required` and\n   `touch /var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch/.gate-scratch-mounted`; `chmod 0700` the mount point.\n   Order matters: creating `required` while unmounted makes admission refuse.\n6. Reclaim the stranded 9.3G on root (mount the volume elsewhere first, or clear the directory\n   before mounting).\n7. Confirm `gate_scratch_mounted` flips to 1 and `disk_used_percent` starts publishing.\n\nDeliberately NOT executed tonight: seven agent sessions are pushing changes and the review-bot is\nactively reviewing them, so there is no quiet window, and the degraded state is stable.\n\n## Scope split: what lands in code, and what only an operator can do\n\nAC1-AC4 are HOST actions on a running production box (format, mount, markers, reclaim). No agent\nperforms them, so they are tagged `[non-codebase]` and are discharged by an operator executing\n`infra/runbooks/gate-scratch-mount.md` and recording the result here. What lands as code is that\nrunbook plus AC5's delivery-gap assertion. Splitting it this way is not a descope: a turnkey,\nverified, rollback-per-step procedure IS the deliverable for AC1-AC4, because the risky part is\nthe sequence, not the individual commands.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] [non-codebase] The gate-scratch volume is formatted, in `/etc/fstab` by UUID, and mounted at\n      `/var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch`. Proven by `mountpoint -q` and `lsblk` showing the fstype.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] Both markers exist on their intended filesystems, created in the safe order, and\n      `chmod 0700` is applied. Proven by `ls` plus confirming they are on different devices.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] `rebar/host:gate_scratch_mounted` publishes 1 and `disk_used_percent` resumes publishing;\n      both alarms return to OK. Proven from CloudWatch, not from the host alone.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] The stranded copy under root is reclaimed and `df /` reflects it.\n- [ ] The DELIVERY GAP is closed, not just this instance: a volume added by `terraform apply` after\n      first boot is either mounted by something that runs post-boot, or its absence is made a\n      loud, actionable signal at apply time. A fix that only mounts this one volume by hand leaves\n      the next one to be discovered the same way — by an alarm, days later."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788648921256046001,"uuid":"c5faa7c4-f058-42fa-85f0-afd85cb72e8f"}